DNS Record          Description                    Function
    A                 Address               Links hostname to IPv4
    AAAA                                    Same, but for IPv6
    CNAME             Canonical Name        Points domain to another or subdomain
    MX                Mail Exchange         Directs emails to mail server
    SOA               Start of Authority    Stores important info about a domain or zone                Zone transfer = Sends DNS recors data from primary to a secondary nameserver
    PTR               Pointer               Correlates IP address with a domain name                    Reverse DNS Lookup = Determines what the domain name is for a given IP address
    TXT               Text                  Adds text into the DNS 
    NS        nameserver            Indicates which DNS nameserver has the Authority            Type of DNS server that stores all the DNS records for a given domain

    Domain name -> IP address = Forward Lookup

    Internal DNS - Allows cloud instances on the same network access each other using internal DNS names
    External DNS - Records created around the domain names from a central authority and used on the public internet

    Time To Live (TTL) - Tells DNS resolver how long to cache a query before requesting a new one
    DNS Resolver/DNS Cache - Makes a local copy of every DNS entry it resolves as the user connects to websites

    DNS uses a recursive strat to perform a look up
        If your local DNS server does not know the answer, it moves up a level and they ask around, and so on.

    Recursive Lookup - DNS server hunts it down and report back to the resolver

    VS

    Iterative Lookup - DNS resolver will continually query DNS servers until it finds the domain IP
